{
  "dom_id": "step-q99266",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}⟨{t}^{2},\\sqrt{2}t,1⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1)}⟨2t,\\sqrt{2}(1-{t}^{2}),-2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively.",
  "worked_solution": "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}⟨{t}^{2},\\sqrt{2}t,1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1)}⟨2t,\\sqrt{2}(1-{t}^{2}),-2t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {t}^{2} & \\sqrt{2}t & 1 \\\\ 2t & \\sqrt{2}(1-{t}^{2}) & -2t\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}t & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}(1-{t}^{2}) & -2t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & 1 \\\\ 2t & -2t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & \\sqrt{2}t \\\\ 2t & \\sqrt{2}(1-{t}^{2})\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}⟨-2\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2}-\\sqrt{2}(1-{t}^{2}),-(-2{t}^{3}-2t),(\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2}(1-{t}^{2})-2\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}⟨-\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2}-\\sqrt{2},2{t}^{3}+2t,-\\sqrt{2}{t}^{4}-\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}⟨-\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1),2t({t}^{2}+1),-\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}⟨-1,\\sqrt{2}t,-{t}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
